p with a bill Azam during the  1980s Abdullah Azzam  Palestinian Muslim brother who is the  founder of a precursor organization of  al-qaeda and so it's not as if he wasn't  acquainted with the issues and he did  advocate for American boycotts and anti  Israeli boycotts in the 1980s as part of  his speech making so I mean to be fair  to him you know he he did speak about  the issue but as he independently even  early but as he independently built up  al-qaeda after his arms assassination in  1989 and as he defined al-qaeda's global  campaign he moved increasingly away from  rhetorical or other concerns involving  the israeli-palestinian conflict it's  notable that to date so far as we know  no cells that we would strictly call  al-qaeda have even attacked Israeli  targets  you know part because the targets are  very hard and whenever they've scouted  them they've basically backed off  Zawahiri and his memoir writes about how  they ended up bombing the egyptian  embassy in Islamabad in 1995 he sent his  team down and first mission was hit the  Americans they looked at the embassy is  said it's too hard to hit they said okay  well then hit the Brits they looked at  the embassy they said it's too hard to  hit and they said okay well then look at  the Egyptians and they said well that we  can do and I think with the Israelis  that you know occasionally they've  they've kind of come off of targets but  I've been Laden's concerns are Saudi I  mean bin Laden I think it has to be  understood as a as a sort of a Saudi  global jihadist and and I think that the  groups around him the Egyptians the  Algerians the Yemenis and others who  have made up the Chechens the Uzbek so I  mean those who have made up the sort of  heart of his hard core whether in the  Soudan phase or in the afghan phase have  been by comparison to other Arab radical  populations relatively disinterested in  the israeli-palestinian conflict and  also one other thing you know after all  Palestinian operate a